The diagnostic node is the empty synthetic shim:

```text
fileName: '/some/sample/path/src/app/some.component.ngtypecheck.ts'
```

That loop repeats. On the fifth exit:

```text
[Error] The Angular Language Service server crashed 5 times in the last 3 minutes.
        The server will not be restarted.
```

`ng serve` for the same project does not report this. `strictTemplates` stays clean.

## Why the compiler stays quiet

Same-file `imports: [HelperDirective]` is resolved as a local class reference. TypeScript does not require `export` for that, and the Angular compiler has no diagnostic that a directive used only in its own file must be exported.

Template checking during a build also never attempts the failing import:

1. The build constructs the compiler with the template type checker disabled, so optional directive-instance operations are skipped. `HelperDirective` has nothing the binding checker must name on its own.

From `packages/compiler-cli` program setup (`@angular/compiler-cli` 21.2):

```js
ticket = freshCompilationTicket(
  program,
  this.options,
  strategy,
  programDriver,
  perfRecorder,
  /* enableTemplateTypeChecker */ false,
  /* usePoisonedData */ false,
);
```

The scope renderer skips those operations unless the flag is on:

```js
render() {
  for (let i = 0; i < this.opQueue.length; i++) {
    const skipOptional = !this.tcb.env.config.enableTemplateTypeChecker;
    this.executeOp(i, skipOptional);
  }
  return this.statements;
}

executeOp(opIndex, skipOptional) {
  const op = this.opQueue[opIndex];
  if (skipOptional && op.optional) {
    return null;
  }
  let res = op.execute();
  // ...
}
```

`TcbDirectiveTypeOpBase.optional` is `true`. The statement only declares the directive instance. With the flag off, `referenceTcbValue` is never called for this helper.

2. Inlining, which would keep the reference in the original file where the class is in scope, is decided only from the component class and used pipes. An exported component plus no unimportable pipes yields `None`, so the block is written to the shim instead of inline.

From `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/tcb_util.ts`:

```js
function requiresInlineTypeCheckBlock(ref, env, usedPipes, reflector) {
  if (!env.canReferenceType(ref)) {
    return TcbInliningRequirement.MustInline;
  } else if (!checkIfGenericTypeBoundsCanBeEmitted(ref.node, reflector, (r) => env.canReferenceType(r))) {
    return TcbInliningRequirement.ShouldInlineForGenericBounds;
  } else if (usedPipes.some((pipeRef) => !env.canReferenceType(pipeRef))) {
    return TcbInliningRequirement.MustInline;
  } else {
    return TcbInliningRequirement.None;
  }
}
```

`ref` here is the component (`SomeComponent`), which is exported. Directives matched in the template are not consulted.

## Why the language service crashes

The extension type-checks through `some.component.ngtypecheck.ts`. Its program driver uses `InliningMode.CopySourceToTcb`:

```js
function createProgramDriver(project) {
  return {
    inliningMode: InliningMode.CopySourceToTcb,
    supportsInlineOperations: false,
    // ...
  };
}
```

`CopySourceToTcb` is supposed to copy the original file into the shim and then emit a bare name for symbols that came from that file. `extractReferenceMetadata` already has that bridge:

```js
// When the compiler operates in `CopySourceToTcb` mode, it creates a separate shim file
// and copies the entire source content of the original file into it. From a pure text
// perspective in that shim file, the original classes are local.
//
// However, during the generation phase here, the compiler is still working with the
// AST nodes of the original file. So, `ref.node.getSourceFile()` returns the original
// file, not the shim file. If we fall through to standard reference resolution below,
// the emitter would assume it needs to generate an import (because the target file and
// source file don't match in AST terms). For non-exported symbols, that auto-import
// would fail.
if (env.copiedSourceOriginPath !== undefined) {
  const refFile = absoluteFromSourceFile(ref.node.getSourceFile());
  if (refFile === env.copiedSourceOriginPath) {
    return {
      name,
      moduleName: null,
      isLocal: true,
      unexportedDiagnostic: null,
      // ...
    };
  }
}
```

That early return never runs for this component. `copiedSourceOriginPath` is set only when inlining was required:

```js
const inliningRequirement = requiresInlineTypeCheckBlock(ref, shimData.file, usedPipes, this.reflector);
if (inliningRequirement !== TcbInliningRequirement.None &&
    (this.inlining === InliningMode.InlineOps || this.inlining === InliningMode.CopySourceToTcb)) {
  this.addInlineTypeCheckBlock(fileData, shimData, ref, meta);
  if (this.inlining === InliningMode.CopySourceToTcb) {
    shimData.file.copiedSourceOriginPath = absoluteFromSourceFile(sourceFile);
  }
} else {
  shimData.file.addTypeCheckBlock(
    ref, meta, shimData.domSchemaChecker, shimData.oobRecorder,
    TcbGenericContextBehavior.UseEmitter, this.reflector,
  );
}
```

`SomeComponent` is exported, so `requiresInlineTypeCheckBlock` returns `None`. The `else` branch generates the block in the shim and tries a real import. `HelperDirective` is not an export, so emit fails.

`referenceTcbValue` then throws instead of recording a diagnostic. From `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/reference_emit_environment.ts`:

```js
referenceTcbValue(ref) {
  if (ref.unexportedDiagnostic !== null || ref.isLocal || ref.moduleName === null) {
    if (ref.unexportedDiagnostic !== null) {
      throw new FatalDiagnosticError(
        ErrorCode.IMPORT_GENERATION_FAILURE,
        this.contextFile,
        makeDiagnosticChain(`Unable to import symbol ${ref.name}.`, [
          makeDiagnosticChain(ref.unexportedDiagnostic),
        ]),
      );
    }
    return new TcbExpr(ref.name);
  }
  return this.referenceExternalSymbol(ref.moduleName, ref.name);
}
```

The stack reaches that throw from `TcbNonGenericDirectiveTypeOp.execute` while rendering the `@for` inside `@if`. `FatalDiagnosticError` is caught around class analysis (decorator metadata, template parse). It is not caught around type-check-block rendering, so the exception kills the Node process.

One related setting is easy to misread. The language service sets `compileNonExportedClasses = false` so non-exported test NgModules are not compilation roots. That does not apply here: the helper is reached through the exported component's `imports` array, and the shim still has to name it.

## Expected behavior

Either of these would match the compiler:

- Treat a directive declared in the same file as the component as a local name in the `.ngtypecheck.ts` shim, including when `requiresInlineTypeCheckBlock` is `None`. The `copiedSourceOriginPath` check above already does this once that path is set.
- If the import still cannot be generated, catch `FatalDiagnosticError` while rendering the type-check block and report a diagnostic. Do not exit the language-server process.

A missing `export` on a file-private directive should not take down the language service. `export class HelperDirective` is a workaround, not a required program change.


